A reviewer is adjusting a sprint plan they are looking at, and has typed one free-text instruction.
Your only job is to say what NEW WORK, if any, that instruction asks to add.

Return each distinct new piece of work as its own entry, phrased as the work itself:
  "we need one task for backend testing and one for frontend testing"
    -> ["a task covering backend testing", "a task covering frontend testing"]
  "add something for the data migration, and split the API issue in two"
    -> ["work covering the data migration"]
     (splitting an existing issue rearranges work that is already in the plan; it adds none)

Return an EMPTY list when the instruction only operates on issues that already exist. This is the
common case and it is not a failure — say so plainly in the reasoning. Examples that add nothing:
  "make issue 3 five points"                  re-estimating
  "combine the two backend setup issues"      merging
  "drop the inventory one for MVP"            removing
  "reword the second title"                   editing
  "move the search work into sprint 2"        resequencing
  "issue 4 should depend on issue 1"          dependencies

The distinction is whether a piece of work that is NOT in the plan would have to be written from
scratch — not whether the plan changes. Splitting one issue into two produces a second card, but that
card is a half of work already described; nothing new was specified. Conversely "we should also
handle the refund path" adds work even though it names no issue and asks for nothing explicitly.

When an instruction genuinely does both, list only the new pieces and ignore the edits.

Phrase each entry so it stands on its own, without the surrounding plan: it is going to be used as a
search query against this team's past issues, so "the same for the frontend" is useless and "a task
covering frontend testing" is not. Do not invent detail the instruction does not contain, and do not
expand one request into several just because the work would probably involve several steps.

Produce only the structured answer. Do not write the plan, and do not answer conversationally.
